//
// Phase C1 operator gate: causal_mask.
//
// Attention scores plus a precomputed triangular mask. There is no device
// design of its own -- it is the element-wise add above with a static
// -10000.0 mask bound as the second operand, which is exactly the claim
// this test exists to keep honest.
//
// Runs opcheck.py on real NPU2 against an FP32 reference: np.isclose over the
// full output at rtol=1.6e-2, zero permitted mismatches. Not cosine similarity
// -- kernel_registry/README.md is explicit that a correlation gate is blind to
// a systematic per-element scale error.
//
// NPU serialization lives one layer down, not in the recipe below. Device
// access is locked on /tmp/npu.lock, which XRTRunner.run_test holds across
// xclbin load and dispatch (python/air/backend/xrt_runner.py:292) -- the same
// inode lit.cfg.py's NPU substitution and KernelCache take, so this interlocks
// with every other NPU job in the repository while the peano compiles still
// overlap. Taking the outer /tmp/mlir-air-npu.lock here instead would DEADLOCK:
// the gate command that invokes this suite already holds that inode, and BSD
// flock(2) treats a second open() of the same file as a foreign lock, so the
// nested acquire would block against its own parent until the timeout expires.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_npu2_causal_mask_peano
// RUN: cd test_npu2_causal_mask_peano
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile clean
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-causal-mask PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s
//
// Both lines, not just the verdict: the first is XRTRunner's own, the second
// names the operator and shape, so a test wired to the wrong operator fails
// here rather than passing quietly.
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] causal_mask [512x512]: PASS
